-- title:
--   Chart functions in the Gauss maximal ideal vanish on C₁
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$; let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a $p$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and let $K$ be the intermediate field $L \subseteq K \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ obtained as the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field $x1FunctionField(Mp)$ of $\Gamma_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $p$ in its maximal ideal and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$, acting on $K$ compatibly with $L$, and let $j \in K$ be the element whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of $q^{-1}\cdot jNumQ$, with $j \neq 0$. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed $A$-algebra field of characteristic $p$. Let $C_1, C_2$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral $k$-schemes, with closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ into the base change to $k$ of the two-chart model $\mathrm{modelTo}\,A\,K\,j$ (whose two charts are the spectra of the subalgebras of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$ and over $A[j^{-1}]$, which is assumed proper over $A$), such that the images of $i_1$ and $i_2$ cover the special fibre, the scheme-theoretic intersection $\mathrm{pullback}\,i_1\,i_2$ is reduced and has exactly $n > 0$ points. Let $w$ be an integral weight-one form for $\Gamma_1(M)$ over $k$ (a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with an integral power series $q$-expansion whose reduction to $k$ is nonzero), let $Mdl_1$ be a curve model over $k$ of the Igusa function field $\mathrm{igusaFunctionFieldX1C}\,k\,M\,w$, obtained by adjoining to the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_1(M)$ over $k$ the inverse of the reduced $q$-expansion of $w$, and let $e_1 : Mdl_1.C \cong C_1$ be an isomorphism over $\operatorname{Spec} k$. Assume that the preimage under $e_1.\mathrm{hom}$ followed by $i_1$ followed by the projection to the two-chart model of the open image of the finite chart $\mathrm{XFin}\,A\,K\,j$ is nonempty, and the Gauss reading hypothesis: for every $a$ in the finite chart algebra and all power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $\bar y \neq 0$ in $k$ and $a\,\bar y_L = \bar x_L$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$, the section of $C_1$ obtained by pulling $a$ back along that composite and transporting its generic germ through $Mdl_1.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$ has Laurent expansion $\bar x / \bar y$ over $k$. Let finally $W_0$ be a valuation subring of $K$ consisting exactly of those $f$ admitting a presentation $f\,\bar y_L = \bar x_L$ with $x, y$ power series over $A$ and $y$ of nonzero reduction. The conclusion: for every point $\xi$ of $C_1$ and every point $y$ of the finite chart $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the chart algebra whose image under $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$ equals the image of $\xi$ in the special fibre of the two-chart model, every element $a$ of the chart algebra whose image in $K$ lies in the maximal ideal of $W_0$ belongs to the prime ideal $y$.
--
--   This is the orientation statement for the Gauss valuation ring in the analysis of the geometric special fibre of the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ at $p$: the component $C_1$ distinguished by the Gauss reading hypothesis sees the finite-chart points that lie over the centre of $W_0$, so that chart functions with non-unit Gauss valuation vanish there. It is used in identifying components of the mod $p$ fibre with minimal primes of the chart algebra.
